No Write tool in this session, so here is the full design.

## Summary of verified findings (several correct the brief)

**Confirmed:**
- `useHasLinkShapeSelected` duck-types on `'url' in props` (`menu-hooks.ts:173-188`); `EditLinkDialog.tsx:33-40` does the same and writes via `editor.updateShapes`. `EditLinkMenuItem` is rendered inside `EditMenuSubmenu` (`tldraw/src/lib/ui/components/menu-items.tsx:350-368`), which `DefaultContextMenuContent` renders — so `CustomContextMenu.tsx:65` picks it up with zero UI code.
- Shape utils feed the schema through `utilsToMap` reading the **static** `props`/`migrations` off the constructor (`@tldraw/editor/src/lib/config/createTLStore.ts:139-149`), so **subclassing `TextShapeUtil` and overriding only `static props` works**. Statics are inherited, so `static migrations = textShapeMigrations` stays as-is and keeps sequence id `com.tldraw.shape.text` (assembled in `processPropsMigrations`, `@tldraw/tlschema/src/recordsWithProps.ts:52-113`). **No conflict** — our url backfill must live in a *separate* sequence, because `processPropsMigrations` asserts `sequenceId === 'com.tldraw.shape.text'` for any sequence attached to the text util (line 68-72).
- `text` is not a core shape (`defaultShapes.ts:15-33` only rejects core types + duplicates), so replacing the default util is legal; appending would throw `Shape type "text" is defined more than once`.
- Retroactive default `true` (`migrate.ts:114`) + `StoreSchema.getMigrationsSince` (`StoreSchema.ts:172-177`) means a new sequence absent from a persisted schema runs from version 0. Confirmed this also covers the cross-tab path: `canvasFileSync.ts:60-79` calls `store.schema.migrateStoreSnapshot`.

**Corrections to the brief — these change the design:**

1. **`.tl-shape` *does* get an intrinsic width/height.** `@tldraw/editor/src/lib/components/Shape.tsx:93-107` sets `width`/`height` inline from `editor.getShapeGeometry(shape).bounds` on every shape container. For text, `getGeometry` returns `getMinDimensions()` × scale (`TextShapeUtil.tsx:74-92`). So `right: 0` lands on the text's right edge with **no wrapper needed** for positioning.

2. **`HyperlinkButton` is NOT exported from `tldraw`.** It is absent from `dist-esm/index.d.mts` (only `RichTextLabel` at 2387 is public). Geo/note/image use it via internal import. We must vendor a ~25-line equivalent using the exported `useEditor` / `useValue` / `stopEventPropagation` (re-exported through `export * from '@tldraw/editor'`, `tldraw/src/index.ts:42`) plus the stock `.tl-hyperlink-button` CSS that is already in `apps/obsidian/src/styles/style.css:1036`.

3. **There IS a z-order problem, but not the one predicted.** `.tl-text-wrapper .tl-text-content { pointer-events: all; z-index: var(--layer-text-content) /* 3 */ }` (`style.css:806-809`). `.tl-shape` has `contain: layout`, so it is a stacking context; the label wrapper is `position:absolute; z-index:auto`, so `.tl-text-content` participates directly in it at z-index 3 and paints **above** a z-index-1 hyperlink button. For geo this is harmless (its `.tl-text-label__inner` is `width: fit-content`, centered, away from the corner); for text the inner box is `textWidth × textHeight` — i.e. the whole shape — so the stock button would be **both visually covered and unclickable**.
   The fix is one CSS rule, not a wrapper div (AGENTS.md:100 forbids inline style hacks): give the text shape's button `z-index: var(--layer-text-content)`. Equal z-index → tree order decides → the button (later sibling) wins over `.tl-text-content`, while the editing overlay `.tl-text-input` at `--layer-text-editor: 4` (`style.css:830-833`) still wins over the button during editing. `RichTextArea` returns `null` unless editing (`RichTextArea.tsx:225`), so nothing overlays the button when idle.

4. **`toSvg` needs no change.** `GeoShapeUtil.toSvg` (`GeoShapeUtil.tsx:255-293`) emits body + text only, never the link. Inheriting `TextShapeUtil.toSvg` is exact parity.

---

## Step 1 — `LinkableTextShapeUtil`

New file: `apps/obsidian/src/components/canvas/shapes/LinkableTextShape.tsx`

```tsx
import {
  T, TextShapeUtil, type TLTextShape, type RecordProps,
  textShapeProps, useEditor, useValue, stopEventPropagation,
} from "tldraw";

export type LinkableTextShape = TLTextShape & {
  props: TLTextShape["props"] & { url: string };
};

const hasUrl = (shape: TLTextShape): shape is LinkableTextShape =>
  "url" in shape.props && typeof shape.props.url === "string";

export class LinkableTextShapeUtil extends TextShapeUtil {
  // `static migrations` is inherited from TextShapeUtil (tldraw's own
  // `com.tldraw.shape.text` sequence). The `url` backfill lives in a separate
  // sequence — processPropsMigrations asserts any sequence attached here is
  // named `com.tldraw.shape.text`, so it cannot be extended in place.
  static override props: RecordProps<LinkableTextShape> = {
    ...textShapeProps,
    url: T.linkUrl,
  };

  override getDefaultProps(): LinkableTextShape["props"] {
    return { ...super.getDefaultProps(), url: "" };
  }

  override component(shape: TLTextShape): React.ReactElement {
    const url = hasUrl(shape) ? shape.props.url : "";
    return (
      <>
        {super.component(shape)}
        {url && <TextHyperlinkButton url={url} />}
      </>
    );
  }
}
```

Notes that matter:
- `super.component(shape)` is what keeps this a true reuse: `useTextShapeKeydownHandler` is module-private (`TextShapeUtil.tsx:336`) and not exported, so re-implementing the label would silently drop Cmd+Enter-to-complete. Calling `super.component` is safe here — `component` is a normal prototype method and the hooks inside it run in the same render (`InnerShape` calls `util.component(...)` inside `useStateTracking`, `Shape.tsx:176-187`). Hook order stays stable because the `url &&` branch renders a component, not a conditional hook.
- `getDefaultProps` widening the return type is legal: the subclass member type must be *assignable* to the base's, and a supertype-with-extra-key is. Same for the `static props` annotation.
- The `hasUrl` guard mirrors tldraw's own defensive `'url' in shape.props` in `NoteShapeUtil.tsx:319` / `VideoShapeUtil.tsx:193`, and covers the window where a record has been loaded but the migration produced `undefined`.
- Do **not** override `toSvg`, `indicator`, `onResize`, `onBeforeUpdate`, or `onEditEnd` — all inherited unchanged.

`TextHyperlinkButton` in the same file, a faithful port of `tldraw/src/lib/shapes/shared/HyperlinkButton.tsx` minus the `classnames` dep (AGENTS.md: prefer existing deps) and minus the inline `mask` style (AGENTS.md:100 — moved to CSS):

```tsx
const TextHyperlinkButton = ({ url }: { url: string }): React.ReactElement => {
  const editor = useEditor();
  const isHidden = useValue("zoomLevel", () => editor.getZoomLevel() < 0.32, [editor]);
  const stopUnlessShift = useCallback<PointerEventHandler>(
    (e) => { if (!editor.inputs.shiftKey) stopEventPropagation(e); },
    [editor],
  );
  return (
    <a
      className={`tl-hyperlink-button${isHidden ? " tl-hyperlink-button__hidden" : ""}`}
      href={url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" title={url} draggable={false}
      onPointerDown={stopUnlessShift} onPointerUp={stopUnlessShift}
    >
      <div className="tl-hyperlink__icon dg-hyperlink__icon" />
    </a>
  );
};
```

The `!editor.inputs.shiftKey` passthrough is deliberate parity: it is what lets shift-drag still move the shape.

## Step 2 — CSS

Append to `apps/obsidian/styles.css` (the Tailwind entry, which already holds the one existing tldraw override at lines 4-9 with an explanatory comment; `scripts/compile.ts:144-167` concatenates it ahead of the vendored `src/styles/style.css`, and our selectors out-specify the vendored ones anyway):

```css
/* tldraw's own HyperlinkButton is not exported, so the icon mask that it sets
   inline lives here instead. */
.dg-hyperlink__icon {
  mask: url("data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg …%3E") center 100% / 100% no-repeat;
  -webkit-mask: url("data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg …%3E") center 100% / 100% no-repeat;
}

/* A text shape's label fills its whole bounding box and paints at
   --layer-text-content (3), so the stock z-index of 1 would leave the link
   button covered and unclickable. Matching z-index — not exceeding it — is
   deliberate: the tie is broken by tree order, so the button (a later sibling)
   wins over the idle label, while the editing overlay .tl-text-input at
   --layer-text-editor (4) still wins over the button. */
.tl-shape[data-shape-type="text"] > .tl-hyperlink-button {
  z-index: var(--layer-text-content);
}
```

Copy the `LINK_ICON` data URI verbatim from `apps/obsidian/node_modules/tldraw/src/lib/shapes/shared/HyperlinkButton.tsx:5-6`.

## Step 3 — Migration

New file: `apps/obsidian/src/components/canvas/shapes/textLinkMigrations.ts`, mirroring `discourseNodeMigrations.ts` exactly (record scope, `filter` on `typeName === "shape" && type === "text"`, `up` backfills only when missing).

- `SEQUENCE_ID_BASE = "com.discourse-graph.obsidian.text-link"` (parallel to `"com.discourse-graph.obsidian.discourse-node"`).
- `createMigrationIds(SEQUENCE_ID_BASE, { addUrl: 1 })`.
- `up`: `if (shape.props.url === undefined) shape.props.url = ""` — semantically identical to tldraw's own `geoShapeVersions.AddUrlProp` (`TLGeoShape.ts:118-124`).
- Leave `retroactive` at its default `true` (`migrate.ts:114`) — that is exactly what makes pre-existing records get the backfill (`StoreSchema.ts:172-177`).
- No `dependsOn` needed: our migration is independent of tldraw's `AddRichText`, and `validateMigrations` would reject a dependency on an id we don't own being missing.

Registered in exactly one place: `apps/obsidian/src/components/canvas/utils/tldraw.ts:68`, `migrations: [discourseNodeMigrations, textLinkMigrations]`.

## Step 4 — The two registration sites → one helper

Yes, extract it. The two lists (`utils/tldraw.ts:55-59` and `TldrawViewComponent.tsx:108-120`) are consumed independently — `createTLStore` uses them for the *schema*, the `Editor` constructor uses them for *rendering* (`Editor.ts:364-388`), and there is **no assertion that they agree**. If only the component list is updated, the button renders but `editor.updateShapes({props:{url}})` throws at validation; if only the store list is updated, links persist but never render. That silent-divergence risk is exactly why this should be one function.

New file: `apps/obsidian/src/components/canvas/shapes/shapeUtils.ts`

```ts
export const buildShapeUtils = (
  options: DiscourseNodeUtilOptions,
): TLAnyShapeUtilConstructor[] => [
  // `text` must be replaced, not appended — checkShapesAndAddCore throws on
  // duplicate shape types.
  ...defaultShapeUtils.filter((util) => util.type !== LinkableTextShapeUtil.type),
  LinkableTextShapeUtil,
  createDiscourseNodeUtil(options),
  createDiscourseRelationUtil(options),
];
```

The two sites already pass an identical context (`{ app, canvasFile, plugin }` — `TldrawView.tsx:119-123` vs `TldrawViewComponent.tsx:110-119`), and `DiscourseNodeUtil.configure(ctx)` and `createDiscourseNodeUtil(ctx)` produce equivalent subclasses (`ShapeUtil.configure`, `ShapeUtil.ts:84-93`, vs `DiscourseNodeShape.tsx:510-515`), so standardising on `createDiscourseNodeUtil` in the helper is behaviour-preserving. Then:
- `utils/tldraw.ts:55-59` → `const customShapeUtils = buildShapeUtils(ctx);`
- `TldrawViewComponent.tsx:108-120` → `const customShapeUtils = buildShapeUtils({ app: plugin.app, canvasFile: file, plugin });`

Keep it unmemoized in the component to preserve today's behaviour (the array and its util classes are already rebuilt every render); do not fold a `useMemo` into this ticket.

Optionally also fix `createRawTldrawFile`'s bare `createTLStore()` (`utils/tldraw.ts:92`) to use the same utils + migrations, so a brand-new empty canvas records the full sequence list. Harmless either way (zero records, retroactive migrations backfill on load) — call it out in review rather than silently changing it.

## Step 5 — Existing handlers

- **Shift/Cmd-click opener** (`TldrawViewComponent.tsx:395-438`, i.e. the `pointer_up` branch): it early-returns unless `shapeAtPoint.type === "discourse-node"`, so a text shape can never reach it. No interaction. Worth knowing: the button's `onPointerDown/Up` intentionally *don't* stop propagation while shift is held, so a shift-click on a linked text shape falls through to this handler, which then ignores it — same as today.
- **"Convert To" special case** (`CustomContextMenu.tsx:37-39`): unchanged. `convertToDiscourseNode.ts:69` only reads `props.richText` and never spreads text props, so a converted node silently drops `url`. That is correct scope-wise (text→discourse-node conversion is explicitly out of scope), but flag it in the PR so nobody treats it as a regression.

## Step 6 — Risks

| Risk | Reality | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| Old canvases fail validation on load | No. The retroactive record-scope migration runs before validation on both paths (`loadSnapshot` at `utils/tldraw.ts:82`, `migrateStoreSnapshot` at `canvasFileSync.ts:71`). | Verify explicitly with a pre-change canvas file. |
