content.text:
Yes — and the check body is only 8 lines, so it's patchable. But there's a consequence that changes the answer.

```js
const validLinkProtocols = new Set(["http:", "https:", "mailto:"]);
const linkUrl = string.check((value) => {
  if (value === "") return;
  const url = parseUrl(value);
  if (!validLinkProtocols.has(url.protocol.toLowerCase())) { throw … }
});
```

Two mechanics first. It has to be the **check body**, not the `Set` — widening the Set alone admits `obsidian://advanced-uri?commandid=…`, the one-click command path. And version-keying `@tldraw/validate@3.14.2` scopes it to Obsidian cleanly, since Roam's 2.4.6 is a separate copy in the store.

**The genuine win is real:** it collapses our custom validator. `isAllowedTextLinkUrl` disappears, `validateUrlForShape`'s text-vs-geo branch disappears, and there's one definition of "valid link" enforced in tldraw's own validator — no drift between ours and theirs. That's a better simplification than the dialog fork would have been.

**But "geo would also get the benefit" doesn't survive contact.** Geo renders the *stock* `HyperlinkButton`, which has no click interception and no override slot. So after the patch a geo shape could *hold* `obsidian://open?vault=Other&file=x`, and clicking it would hand the URI straight to the OS handler — no `preventDefault`, no `resolveObsidianUrlToFile` vault check. Geo would accept page links and then behave **worse** with them than text does: an OS round-trip that defocuses Obsidian, and a cross-vault URL would switch the reader's vault.

That's acceptance without handling, which is arguably a trap rather than a feature — and it's the same hole I closed for text in `5253da0d`.

So it's really three options:

| | Patch surface | Geo gets |
|---|---|---|
| **A. Don't patch the validator** (today) | 1 pkg, 2 files | nothing; our validator stays |
| **B. Patch validator only** | 2 pkgs, 4 files | *accepts* page links, opens them via the OS, no vault check |
| **C. Patch validator + `HyperlinkButton`** | 2 pkgs, 6 files | genuine parity — and we could delete our vendored button |

C is the only one that actually answers Michael's "consistently for both text and geo". It's also the only one where the export question you started with pays off: once the button can intercept, reusing it becomes possible. The cost is a behaviour patch on a shared component, which is the most upgrade-fragile thing we'd own — and it all dies on the next tldraw bump regardless.

My recommendation is **C if we're patching at all, otherwise A** — B is the worst of both, more patch surface for a degraded geo experience.

Before I build anything: C means patching a component's behaviour, not just flipping a predicate. Do you want me to prototype it so we can see the real diff, or put A-vs-C to Michael first since consistency was his ask?

